---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bug report privacy"
  description="What a Hew bug report contains, where it's stored, who reads it, and how to have one deleted."
>
  <div class="container privacy-page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Bug reports</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        What Help ▸ Report Bug… sends, where it goes, and how long it's kept.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What's in a report</h2>
      <p>
        A report can include the description you write, an optional email
        address, your app version and system details, the current session's
        recorded steps, the files the session took in (imported or opened
        files, and textures and library items you added), a file that
        failed to open, your model file, and a tail of the diagnostic log.
        Each of those is a separate checkbox in the dialog, on by default,
        with its size and a preview shown next to it. Untick anything before
        sending.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recorded steps and the model file carry your model's geometry.
        Imported files are those original files byte for byte, such as a
        SketchUp <code>.skp</code> or a photo used as a texture. When a file
        won't open or import, the next report you make, including one from
        the crash screen, offers that file too, byte for byte and under its
        own name, ticked like the other rows, so the problem can be
        reproduced. Untick all of these to send only your description,
        system details, and log.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the desktop app, every mention of your home folder in the log
        tail, a crash message, or the document name is replaced with
        <code>~</code> before the preview is shown, so the preview is what
        gets sent. With Debug Mode on in Settings, one more row appears:
        the raw mouse and keyboard events of the session, off the list
        entirely otherwise.
      </p>

      <h2>Nothing leaves your machine until you click Send</h2>
      <p>
        Opening the dialog sends nothing by itself. Only clicking
        <strong>Send report</strong> submits it, compressed. A report over
        1 GB after compression is sent without the model file, then
        without imported files, then without the file that didn't open; the
        dialog marks each one it leaves out, and the report says which. A
        file that didn't open and was left out still goes by name, size, and
        the error it failed with, never its contents.
        <strong>Save to file…</strong> writes everything you ticked to a file
        on your machine instead, and <strong>Cancel</strong> sends nothing at
        all.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it's stored</h2>
      <p>
        Reports are stored on Cloudflare, inside Hew's own account — not on
        a public bug tracker, and not visible to other users. A report is
        never posted anywhere public unless you separately choose to open a
        GitHub issue afterward, which never carries an attachment.
      </p>

      <h2>Who reads it</h2>
      <p>
        Only the Hew maintainer, through a private, access-controlled page.
        Sending a report also sends the maintainer one email with a summary
        and a link to it.
      </p>

      <h2>How long it's kept</h2>
      <p>
        Reports are kept for 90 days, then deleted automatically. If you'd
        like yours deleted sooner, contact the maintainer with your report
        ID (the <code>HEW-XXXX-XXXX</code> code shown after sending) and ask
        for it to be removed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="following-a-report">Following a report</h2>
      <p>
        The Report Bug dialog has a checkbox, <strong>Let me know what happens
        with this report</strong>. It is ticked by default, and Hew remembers
        your choice. If you untick it, Hew keeps nothing for that report.
      </p>
      <p>
        If it is ticked, Hew keeps a private random key for the report on
        this computer, along with the report ID, the date sent, your Hew
        version, and the first line of the description. On the desktop app
        these go in a file named <code>followed-reports.json</code> in the
        app's config folder, readable only by your user account. On the web
        they go in the browser's local storage for the app. The key is not an
        account or an identity, and no email address is asked for.
      </p>
      <p>
        When Hew starts, about 15 seconds after launch, it checks each
        followed report that is still unresolved and was last checked more
        than six hours ago. Each check is one small request to the same
        private bug-report service, with the key in a request header and never
        in the address. Checking stops on its own once you have seen a final
        status (Fixed or Closed), or after a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Help ▸ Sent Reports…</strong> lists your followed reports
        with their status and shows the conversation with Hew's developer. It
        lets you reply, attach a file, check now, or forget a report. Replies
        and attachments go to the same private service that receives reports,
        and the developer is emailed about each one, the way a new report
        emails them. Replies are plain text, up to 4,000 characters, 20 a day
        per report, and 200 messages per thread.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Forget this report</strong> deletes the key on this computer
        and asks the service to delete the conversation at once. The report
        itself stays with the developer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The service keeps a conversation until 30 days after you have seen a
        final status, or one year after the last activity, whichever comes
        first. That can be longer than the 90 days a report itself is kept,
        because a "fixed in version" note may come later.
      </p>
      <p>
        Following belongs to one computer or browser profile, so a report
        cannot be followed from another machine. Clearing browser data clears
        the key on the web; the service removes the conversation later. The
        self-hosted web build cannot send reports, so it has nothing to
        follow. The desktop app already makes a silent request to GitHub at
        launch to check for updates. The follow check is smaller, is chosen
        for each report, and ends by itself.
      </p>

      <h2>What's never stored</h2>
      <p>
        Your IP address is never stored. A submission is only checked
        against a same-day, salted hash of it, so a burst of reports from
        one source can be rate-limited without keeping the address itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  .page-head {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .page-head .intro {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
